  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry Saturday with a mix of clouds and sun, \nbut there remains a chance for some widely scattered showers or t-\nstorms. \n\nA warmer airmass remains overhead thanks to brief mid level ridging. \nThis will continue to deliver highs in the mid to upper 80s Saturday \nand Sunday. The ridge axis crosses SNE this morning then begins to \nbreak down slowly over the next 24-36 hours. For this reason, \ndespite the surface high pressure nearby, introduced a slight chance \nof rain showers or a thunderstorm this afternoon, mainly over the \ninterior. That being said, the forecast for a mostly dry day \npersists, with just some widely scattered showers or thunderstorms \ngiven a few 1000 J/kg of CAPE. Any convection that does fire up has \nthe potential to induce a quick downpour given PWATs still over 1\". \nFor most locations we'll see a mix of sun and diurnal low level \nclouds. High clouds will be thickening and lowering through the day \nahead of the approaching system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front brings the return of heavy rain and \ngusty winds Sunday night and Monday, perhaps lingering into Tuesday. \n\nThe mid level ridge continues to break down on Sunday as an \nanomolous mid-level low digs into the Ohio Valley inducing a \ndeepening surface low which itself very slowly tracks northeast. \nThis wave moves into New England by Monday, accompanied by and \npreceded by an anomalous subtropical moisture plume with PWATS over \n2\" (2+SD above normal). Isentropically forced rain arrives by late \nSunday evening (west) or Sunday night (east) as warm advection takes \nhold. The heaviest rainfall rates likely occur on Monday as the cold \nfront advances at the surface. This, combined with forcing for \nascent from a 35-45kt LLJ and placement under the RRQ of a 120kt \n300mb jet will lead to moderate to heavy rainfall across SNE. Still \ntoo soon to say where the heaviest amounts will be or how much, but \nensemble guidance is not excited about potential for 2+ inches \nthrough Tuesday morning despite some deterministic guidance \nsuggesting it. This will come into focus as we get into range of \nmore high resolution guidance. The previously mentioned LLJ \nwill bring gusty winds on Monday, especially over the \nCape/islands and over the waters. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Periodic rain chances will be with us for much of \nnext week. \n\nLow confidence forecast when it comes to how much, if any, of a \nbreak we see in wet weather between the exiting shortwave Monday \nnight/early Tuesday and the next. That being said, no sprawling or \npersistent high pressure ridge is in the cards so periodic rain \nchances likely persist.",
